  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled, checks every minute, and its first run finished with status `ok`. That only tells us the query ran without an error. I couldn't confirm that the 5xx filter actually matches any log entries, because the API key variable was empty in my shell.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view has one entry per web request, with these main fields:\n- **When:** `timestamp`, which is the time field.\n- **The request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer` and `agent`.\n- **The result:** `response`, the HTTP status code (stored as text), and `bytes`.\n- **The visitor:** `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest` (source and destination country), `geo.coordinates` and `machine.os`.\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `hour_of_day` and `memory`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-911ae7f4 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels and defaults to the last 7 days. Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-911ae7f4-web-logs-overview-d0dbac6ed1fb\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of each response code's share.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs with request count and bytes.\n- A bar chart of requests by source country.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-911ae7f4 5xx spike\"** (id `0b95d0e4-4f3b-48a3-ba9f-4a1fa6778132`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" and fires when more than 10 arrive within 5 minutes. The rule has no notification actions, so when it fires the alert shows up in Kibana but nobody is notified. You'd need to add a connector, such as Slack or email, for that.",
